o highly  infectious to other people relative to  the other kind leprosy overall is not  actually that infectious but this is the  more infectious one of the two and it's  also sometimes called multipath Soleri  Vasiliy refers to bacillus which is the  rod shape of the micro bacterium and  molti just meaning significant bacterial  growth so there's there lots of the  micro bacterium inside the macrophages  which are the cells where they will they  can actually replicate enzyme so now  let's talk about tuberculoid leprosy  also known as T lab this is the less  severe kind it progresses more slowly  there are fewer lesions there's less  bacterial growth and overall tissue and  nerve damage and people with T lab are  fairly rare so it's it's much less  severe than the other lab why is it so  much less severe well that's because  it's characterized by a different type  of immune response and that is that  stronger cell mediated response  specifically via some a group of T cells  called cytotoxic T cells so cytotoxic T  cells also known as cd8 t-cells  sometimes referred to as killer T cells  cytotoxic T cells are ourself that will  actually kill infected cells so the  Mycobacterium leprae as it is  replicating inside a cell Sian's toxic T  cells can come along and kill that cell  stopping that replication process and  that's why it's a much more effective  immune response against this particular  pathogen so T lab has a lower end  activity to others relative to L lab  sometimes it's referred to as Posse  basilar e posse come from the Latin for  few or small so again few bacilli few  bacteria in those macrophages now you  might be asking yourself well what what  leads to L lab versus T lab because they  are the same pathogen both of them are  leprosy both of them are cousin  Mycobacterium leprae  and it turns out that what controls  whether a person develops L left or T  lab during a Mycobacterium leprae  infection what controls which type they  get is actually their genetics so a  person's particular genetic profile  which genes they have which genes are  active first its inactive is what  actually controls whether they will  proceed with a lab or a T lab and so if  you are interested in learning about  some more related topics you can check  out my playlist on epidemiology and  disease and immunology these playlists  have videos on things like antibiotic  resistant bacteria and goats  two lives and disease vectors and  antibody structure and function and a  lot of other pretty cool topics so check  those out and thanks for watching  [Music]
 

